// Lesson 1.5 — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime & Tuning
// First-introduction lesson: FULL scaffold (walkthrough level 1).
//
// `vector.normalized` is a NEW concept (vector magnitude), so it earns the
// full treatment. Note what this lesson deliberately does NOT do: it does not
// add a Rigidbody2D and does not change the movement method. That decision
// belongs to 2.2 — 2.1 gives the Player its Collider2D and Rigidbody2D (for
// coin triggers), and 2.2 is where the student discovers Translate still
// ignores walls and chooses a replacement. Adding a rigidbody here would
// contradict both lessons.
//
// STRUCTURE (tightening pass): the clicks are "In the Editor" — open the file,
// feel the diagonal bug in play mode, tune the Move Speed box while the game
// runs, bisect to a number, enter it in edit mode, commit. The file work is
// "In the Code": the four-technique table, the do-not-switch warning, the
// socratic and the walkthrough. The old "Read the Docs", "What deltaTime is
// really doing" and "Tuning Lab" labels are absorbed into those two.
//
// CULLED FOR REPETITION AND PRE-EXPLANATION: the "What deltaTime is really
// doing" activity (three paragraphs) explained frame-rate independence that the
// technique table's velocity row, the walkthrough's `movement` note and 1.3's
// Update() row had each already said — the one clause worth keeping is now in
// the walkthrough line where the student types the multiplication. The
// "Note what MovePosition buys you" paragraph restated its own table row and
// pre-explained 2.2, and is gone. The old "Read Function-Level Examples" block
// (racing kart, pendulum clock) was three more unanchored snippets and is gone.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to the diagonal-speed bug. Level 1,
// so every part is given in full. The split is the four parts of 1.4 with the
// resize wedged in the middle on purpose: the vector 1.4 built is correct as a
// direction and wrong as a length, so "make it the right length" has to be its
// own part, and it can only be its own part if speed is kept OUT of the
// direction until the last one. That ordering is the lesson — and it is why the
// walkthrough is a single step (the fix is three lines in one place in Update())
// while the split above it stays at four.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'My character moves faster on the diagonal than in a straight line. The movement from 1.4 ' +
    'already works, and that is what makes the wrongness hard to see.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Read which way the player is pushing',
      does: 'The two axis reads from 1.4, assigned to the `inputX` and `inputY` fields you already declared. Unchanged — nothing here is the bug.',
      doc: 'the `Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")` row in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Build a direction vector out of the two numbers',
      does: 'Pack both axes into one `Vector2`: pure direction, with no speed and no time multiplied in. Its length is whatever the input happens to be — the whole problem.',
      doc: 'the `Vector2` row in the quick reference, and the vector you built in 1.4.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Rescale that vector to a length of exactly 1',
      does: '`direction = direction.normalized;` keeps the arrow pointing the same way and throws its length away, so "up and right" is `(0.707, 0.707)` instead of `(1, 1)`. The fix is general, not a special case for diagonals.',
      doc: 'the `(a - b).normalized` row in the quick reference — direction, length exactly 1.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Scale that unit direction into a distance, and apply it',
      does: 'One multiplication: `direction * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime`. Because the direction is always length 1, this distance is identical whichever way you face. Then the Transform applies it, as in 1.4.',
      doc: 'the `Time.deltaTime` and translate rows in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'The new part sits in the middle rather than at the end. The vector from 1.4 was two numbers ' +
    'stapled together, which is fine until you do arithmetic with them: (1, 0) is length 1, (0, 1) ' +
    'is length 1, but (1, 1) is 1.41 — so two keys moved you 41% further. Keep direction and ' +
    'distance in separate parts and the fix is one line.',
  sizeTest:
    'Is "which way" still in the same part as "how far"? If you cannot point at the one line where ' +
    'speed is applied, the two are still tangled.',
  turns: [
    'The fix was one line, and it landed in the middle part rather than the last one. Why does a ' +
      'bug get easy to find exactly when the problem has been split into the right parts?',
  ],
};

const WALK = {
  ask: 'why does my character move faster diagonally, and how do I fix it?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro: 'You have working movement from 1.4. This replaces two of those lines; the fields and the input reads do not change.',
  level: 1,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Separate direction from speed',
      lead: 'In 1.4 you multiplied the input by speed in two separate places. Pull those apart: first work out which way to go, then decide how far.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — inside Update(), replacing the two displacement lines from 1.4.', what: '' },
        { code: '        Vector2 direction = new Vector2(inputX, inputY);', what: 'A vector pointing whichever way the player holds. Pure **direction** — no speed, no time — and its length is whatever the input happens to be: D alone gives `(1, 0)`, but W and D together give `(1, 1)`, which is **1.41 long, not 1**. That extra 41% is the bug you can feel.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        direction = direction.normalized;', what: 'The fix: `.normalized` keeps the vector pointing the same way but **rescales it to a length of exactly 1**, so "up and right" becomes `(0.707, 0.707)`.', why: 'There is no second `Vector2` in front of `direction` — the variable already exists, so this line **assigns** to it, as `inputX` did in 1.3.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        Vector2 movement = direction * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime;', what: 'Scales that unit direction up to a real distance: one second of travel (`moveSpeed`), cut down to one frame (`Time.deltaTime`).', why: 'Because `direction` is always length 1, and `Time.deltaTime` is the seconds the last frame took, this is the same real-world speed at 30 FPS and at 240 FPS.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — unchanged from 1.4: the finished distance goes to the Transform.', what: '' },
        { code: '        transform.Translate(movement);', what: 'Hands the distance to the Player\'s own Transform, which adds it to the current position. Unchanged from 1.4.' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;

    private float inputX;
    private float inputY;

    void Update()
    {
        inputX = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
        inputY = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");

        Vector2 direction = new Vector2(inputX, inputY);
        direction = direction.normalized;

        Vector2 movement = direction * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime;

        transform.Translate(movement);
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'The only lines that should differ from 1.4 are the ones building `direction` and `movement` — fields, input reads and translate call are unchanged.',
};

const body = `<h1>1.5 — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime &amp; Tuning</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">Your character moves, and one thing about that is wrong: hold two direction keys at once and it travels 41% faster. Today you find out why, fix it in one line, and tune the number that decides how the character feels.</p>

${renderAskBanner('why does my character move faster diagonally, and how do I fix it?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('1.5', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span><span class="activity-time">~20 min</span></div>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Scripts</code>, and open <code>PlayerController.cs</code> — the file you built in 1.4.</li>
        <li><strong>Game view →</strong> press <strong>Play</strong> and hold <strong>D</strong> for two seconds, then <strong>W and D together</strong>. The diagonal covers visibly more ground — you can see the bug before you write anything.</li>
        <li><strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select the <strong>Player</strong>, then <strong>Inspector → Move Speed</strong>. Change the number <em>while the game runs</em> and walk again: too low is sluggish, too high is twitchy.</li>
        <li><strong>Inspector → Move Speed</strong> again, bisecting toward a number that feels right for a world this size — <code>4</code> to <code>7</code> is a good starting range. Write it down.</li>
        <li><strong>Game view →</strong> stop the game, then enter that number in the same box in edit mode so it persists. Then <strong>GitHub Desktop →</strong> commit and push the fix and the tuned speed.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Play mode is a dream, edit mode is reality</strong> (1.2). A value changed while the Play button is blue resets when you stop: play mode to <em>find</em> your number, edit mode to <em>keep</em> it.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Code</span><span class="activity-time">~15 min</span></div>
    <p>Unity offers four ways to move an object, each because it handles something the others handle badly. You use the first today.</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>How it works</th><th>Needs a Rigidbody2D?</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>transform.Translate(vector)</code></td><td><strong>Offsets coordinates directly</strong>, skipping the physics engine.</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>Simple top-down games, moving UI, linear projectiles</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Rigidbody2D.MovePosition(position)</code></td><td><strong>Drives the body to a target position</strong>, so whatever is in the way gets a say: a <strong>Dynamic</strong> body is stopped by a solid collider, a Kinematic one arrives anyway.</td><td>Yes — <strong>Dynamic</strong> if you want a wall to stop it</td><td>Moving platforms and scripted set pieces that must arrive at an exact position</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity = vector</code></td><td><strong>Assigns a velocity</strong> and lets the physics engine move the body. The number is per <em>second</em>, not per frame.</td><td>Yes — <strong>Dynamic</strong> if you want a wall to stop it</td><td>Snappy platformers, arcade physics, momentum across surfaces</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Rigidbody2D.AddForce(vector)</code></td><td><strong>Applies a physical push</strong>, factoring in mass, drag and inertia.</td><td>Yes — <strong>Dynamic</strong>; a Kinematic body ignores forces</td><td>Jumping, thrusters, ice physics, knockback</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>Do not switch methods today.</strong> Three of these need a Rigidbody2D your Player does not have, and you will not add one yet: 2.1 adds it for a different purpose (coins) and 2.2 is where it stops you walking through walls.
    </div>
    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Choosing the Right Tool for the Job</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You proved in <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a> that the translate call walks through walls, while <code>Rigidbody2D.MovePosition(position)</code> is stopped by one — on a <strong>Dynamic</strong> body. What does that call consult that the Transform never does?</li>
            <li>Three of these four need a component your Player does not have. What would you add, and where — on the Player, or on the child Sprite?</li>
            <li>Why would a spaceship drifting through space prefer <code>Rigidbody2D.AddForce(vector)</code> to the translate call?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Compare four ways to move an object: <code>transform.Translate(vector)</code>, <code>Rigidbody2D.MovePosition(position)</code>, <code>Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity</code>, <code>Rigidbody2D.AddForce(vector)</code></li>
        <li>Say which of the four need a Rigidbody2D, which body type a wall can stop, and what moving along a path does differently from a teleport</li>
        <li><strong>Explain, in your own words, why (1, 1) is a longer vector than (1, 0) — and what <code>.normalized</code> does about it</strong></li>
        <li>Explain how <code>Time.deltaTime</code> produces the same real-world speed at different frame rates</li>
        <li>Explain the relationship between a code default and a serialized Inspector value</li>
        <li>Tune your speed while the game runs, save it in edit mode, and commit</li>
    </ul>
</div>

